This README briefly explains how to set up a help set and incorporate it into 
the editor, presumably as part of a plugin.  

-- Required Files --
First you will need the files which specify how to structure and where to find 
the corresponding html files with the actual help content.  The first file you 
need is the HelpSet file itself, "name".hs.  For simplicity's sake just copy 
any file with that extension that you can find.  In the file you will need 
only change a few lines:

<title><!-- Put your title here --></title>

<mapref location="Map.jhm"/>  
<!-- replace Map.jhm with the name of your mapfile -->

<view>
    <name>TOC</name>
    <label>Table Of Contents</label>
    <type>javax.help.TOCView</type>
    <data><!-- Put the path to your table of contents file here --></data>
</view>

<view>
    <name>Index</name>
    <label>Index</label>
    <type>javax.help.IndexView</type>
    <data><!-- Put the path to your index file here --></data>
</view>

The index and TOC (Table of Contents) files should be .xml files.  Again if 
you copy a file ending in TOC.xml or Index.xml and just change it and you 
should be fine.  The important part of these files are lines like

<tocitem text="&quot;File&quot; Options">
    <!-- tocitem inside tocitem for tree hierarchy -->
    <tocitem text="New" target="new_xml"/>
    <tocitem text="Open" target="open_xml"/>
    <tocitem text="Save" target="save_xml"/>
</tocitem>

and 

<indexitem text="Open" target="open_xml"/>

In particular target="" refers to the string ID of one of the help files.  The 
map file ("name".jhm) converts ID's to filenames (or rather, URLs).  Copy a 
map file and then change lines like this:

<mapID target="open_xml" url="filemenu/open_xml.html" />

so that the target is one of your ID's and the URL is the appropriate help 
file for that target.  If you are using the perl script for generation, then 
just remember this filename as you'll need it when you make your generation 
file.  (See "Generation of HTML from templates using Perl" below.)

-- Incorporating Help --
New help sets can be included via the command line if a plugin supports it. 
The standard editor does not provide support for additional help sets.  The 
script editor always includes $XMLEDITHOME/plugins/help/script/ScriptHelp.hs, 
and supports additional help sets with the "script-hs=" command line argument, 
to be followed immediately by the path (relative or absolute) to a help set 
which you want included.  Now that help set will be viewable in the Java Help
window.  For delivery, the process is the same except the always-included help
set is $XMLEDITHOME/plugins/help/delivery/DeliveryHelp.hs and the argument 
for including additional help sets is "deliv-hs=".  ScriptHelp.hs and 
DeliveryHelp.hs should be added to if and only if the help files added deal 
with the core functionality of the script or delivery plugin and not with a 
particular xml file.  In those cases adding a separate help set with 
"script-hs=" or "deliv-hs=" would be best.

-- Generation of HTML from templates using Perl --
The Perl script build_help_html.pl generates html files as per the user's 
specifications given a fixed filename for the template file and the content 
file.  The template file is an HTML file with comments in key locations where 
significant content will be added.  The content file is an ASCII text file 
from which the contents of specific HTML files are gathered.  It should 
conform to the following pattern:

             -- generate_html.txt --
1 line       The name of the file to generate (relative or absolute)
1 line       The title to appear in the html
1 line       The text to appear descriping the title (all on one line)
0..n lines   Additional lines designating parameters or other sub-info
1 blank line	
repeat

The template file and the content file should reside in the same directory as 
the .hs, .jhm, TOC, and index files, and should be called template.html and 
generate_html.txt, respectively.  Then, in that directory, run 
$XMLEDITHOME/help/build_help_html.pl.  Now that all the files are complete, 
run jhindexer in that same directory listing after it the names of all 
directories in which your help .html files reside (including . if you put 
any .html files there).  For now, jhindexer resides in 
~ahryat/jh1.1/javahelp/bin.  Also, be warned it uses ksh so you must have 
that shell to run it.

